><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20250702124527+10'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20250702124547+10'00'" name="67ab1160-3a7a-4381-9cbb-c1ffd060a1d5" page="5" coords="308.842010,158.801865,540.969421,158.801865,308.842010,147.642181,540.969421,147.642181,296.886993,146.846848,540.974121,146.846848,296.886993,135.687164,540.974121,135.687164,296.886993,134.891846,540.963806,134.891846,296.886993,123.732162,540.963806,123.732162,296.886993,122.936844,540.973877,122.936844,296.886993,111.777161,540.973877,111.777161,296.886993,110.981842,540.963867,110.981842,296.886993,99.822159,540.963867,99.822159,296.886993,99.025848,540.973999,99.025848,296.886993,87.866165,540.973999,87.866165,296.886993,87.070847,540.973816,87.070847,296.886993,75.911163,540.973816,75.911163,296.886993,75.115845,540.963989,75.115845,296.886993,63.956161,540.963989,63.956161,40.847000,38.685867,62.842773,38.685867,40.847000,28.309143,62.842773,28.309143,309.512024,38.685867,540.971313,38.352859,309.512024,28.309143,540.971313,28.309143" rect="40.522720,27.984863,541.322876,159.150604" subject="Comment on Text" title="Alexander Gibson"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-16.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>&#xD;</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Despite our study methods, it is likely that MSK com-plaint concealment continued among trainees to some extent because of the fear of unwanted career events. A strength of our research is that we used an embedded military physiother-apist to attempt to overcome this barrier, and we recommend this as continued practice for ongoing surveillance. Establish-ing rapport with personnel is necessary for accurate surveil-lance and to encourage trainees to seek health care when needed.</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-9.3pt;text-align:left;color:#0000FF;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>20 </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Further to this practice, future injury surveillance could include anonymous reporting methods if embedding military clinicians is not possible and if reporting behaviors are felt to impede data collection.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="5" rect="583.937012,66.801865,767.937012,158.801865"
/></highlight
></annots
